  "notes": "The producer of the modern Memphis chant hit, and a producer's model is his workflow. The signature is the **\"menacing key loop\"**: a 2-bar piano or synth figure over a static i with *one chromatic upper-voice move* and no cadence, which is authored as `impliedByRiff` plus `voicing.topVoiceStepwise` true (the chromatic move) plus `melody.halfStepDissonance` 0.34, with `chordDurationBeats` and three `harmonicRhythm` values carrying the chord part's variety instead of progressions he does not write. The drums are the documented \"bass, kick drums and cowbells\": continuous 16th cowbell at 7–11 a bar and 0 dB, clap layered onto the snare on beat 3, and **flat, loud velocities** — accent 106–120, main 92–104, `velocityVar` 0.06 and `quantizeStrength` 0.97, the tightest grid in this batch. The low end is explicitly a rhythm part supporting the chant, not a melodic line: `followRootsProb` 0.97, `glideProb` 0.03, no passing tones. The long rolls into each hook are carried by `pitchWalk` 5 over an 8/16-bar fill cadence. ⚠ The research says there is no countermelody — the arrangement's second melody is the group chant — so it sits at the engine's floor (0.3, two styles) and is the first layer to mute. ⚠ `medium`, per the research's high-medium: the beat description, drum palette, chart facts and his own working method are sourced; **the numeric velocity and density values are the inferred part**. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts. ⚠ **The countermelody reached only 950 distinct answers in 1000 seeds against a floor of 980**, so it was given range: densityRatio 0.45. A counter is combinatorial in the styles it may pick, its density against the melody, and the phrase length it answers inside — one style over a thin density inside a single phrase length has almost nothing a seed can vary.",
